### Animating with ScrollTrigger and Pinning (Orange Panel)

Source: https://github.com/itziklerner-pag/gsap-docs-v3/blob/main/Plugins/ScrollTrigger/static.scrollerProxy().md

Animates an element while pinning the trigger element to the viewport. The animation progresses as the user scrolls through a defined range, with scrub ensuring it matches scroll speed.

```javascript
gsap.from(".line-2", {
  scrollTrigger: {
    trigger: ".orange",
    scrub: true,
    pin: true,
    start: "top top",
    end: "+=100%",
    onUpdate() {
      console.log("Update")
    }
  },
  scaleX: 0, 
  transformOrigin: "left center", 
  ease: "none"
});
```

--------------------------------

### Advanced Timeline Animation with ScrollTrigger

Source: https://github.com/itziklerner-pag/gsap-docs-v3/blob/main/Plugins/ScrollTrigger/index.md

This example demonstrates a complex scroll-based animation using a GSAP timeline. It features timeline pinning, start/end points, smooth scrubbing, and snapping to labels. The animation progresses as the user scrolls through the '.container'.

```javascript
let tl = gsap.timeline({
    // yes, we can add it to an entire timeline!
    scrollTrigger: {
        trigger: '.container',
        pin: true, // pin the trigger element while active
        start: 'top top', // when the top of the trigger hits the top of the viewport
        end: '+=500', // end after scrolling 500px beyond the start
        scrub: 1, // smooth scrubbing, takes 1 second to "catch up" to the scrollbar
        snap: {
            snapTo: 'labels', // snap to the closest label in the timeline
            duration: { min: 0.2, max: 3 }, // the snap animation should be at least 0.2 seconds, but no more than 3 seconds (determined by velocity)
            delay: 0.2, // wait 0.2 seconds from the last scroll event before doing the snapping
            ease: 'power1.inOut' // the ease of the snap animation ("power3" by default)
        }
    }
});

// add animations and labels to the timeline
tl.addLabel('start')
    .from('.box p', { scale: 0.3, rotation: 45, autoAlpha: 0 })
    .addLabel('color')
    .from('.box', { backgroundColor: '#28a92b' })
    .addLabel('spin')
    .to('.box', { rotation: 360 })
    .addLabel('end');
```

--------------------------------

### Horizontal Scroll Animation with ScrollTrigger

Source: https://github.com/itziklerner-pag/gsap-docs-v3/blob/main/HelperFunctions/helpers/getScrollPosition.md

Creates a horizontal scrolling effect where panels move across the screen. Uses `scrub` for smooth animation tied to scroll.

```javascript
let sections = gsap.utils.toArray(".panel");

let scrollTween = gsap.to(sections, {
    xPercent: -100 * (sections.length - 1),
    ease: "none", // <-- IMPORTANT!
    scrollTrigger: {
      trigger: ".container",
      pin: true,
      scrub: 0.1,
      //snap: directionalSnap(1 / (sections.length - 1)),
      end: "+=3000"
    }
  });
```

--------------------------------

### Simple ScrollTrigger.batch() Example

Source: https://github.com/itziklerner-pag/gsap-docs-v3/blob/main/Plugins/ScrollTrigger/static.batch().html

This example demonstrates how to use ScrollTrigger.batch() to apply animations to elements as they enter and leave the viewport. It logs the number of elements entering or leaving.

```javascript
ScrollTrigger.batch(".box", {
  onEnter: (elements, triggers) => {
    gsap.to(elements, { opacity: 1, stagger: 0.15 });
    console.log(elements.length, "elements entered");
  },
  onLeave: (elements, triggers) => {
    gsap.to(elements, { opacity: 0, stagger: 0.15 });
    console.log(elements.length, "elements left");
  },
});
```

--------------------------------

### Batch Animating Multiple Elements on Scroll

Source: https://github.com/itziklerner-pag/gsap-docs-v3/blob/main/Plugins/ScrollTrigger/static.batch().md

This snippet demonstrates how to use ScrollTrigger.batch() to animate multiple elements ('.card') when they enter the viewport. It applies a stagger effect to the animations.

```javascript
// https://greensock.com/docs/v3/Plugins/ScrollTrigger/static.batch()

ScrollTrigger.batch(".card", {
  // interval: 0.1, // time window (in seconds) for batching to occur. 
  // batchMax: 3,   // maximum batch size (targets)
  onEnter: batch => gsap.to(batch, {autoAlpha: 1, stagger: 0.1}),
  // also onLeave, onEnterBack, and onLeaveBack
  // also most normal ScrollTrigger values like start, end, etc.
});
```

